body:
The [check if an access between two browsing contexts should be reported](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#coop-check-access-report) operation can be called with the `accessed` browsing context being null.

Here's an example scenario where this happens:
```html
<!doctype html><script>
window.onload = function() {
    let iframe = document.querySelector("iframe")
    let iframeWindow = iframe.contentWindow;
    iframe.remove();
    iframeWindow.foo;
}
</script><iframe></iframe>
```

After the call to `iframe.remove();` above, the `iframeWindow` no longer has an associated browsing context. (This happens because the iframe removing steps invokes [destroy a child navigable](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/document-sequences.html#destroy-a-child-navigable), which ends up severing the link between the iframe's document and its browsing context.

Yet when evaluating `iframeWindow.foo`, we end up in [7.2.3.7 [[Get]] ( P, Receiver )](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#windowproxy-get) where the following happens:

1. Let W be the value of the [[[Window]]](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-windowproxy-window) internal slot of this.

2. [Check if an access between two browsing contexts should be reported](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#coop-check-access-report), given the [current global object](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#current-global-object)'s [browsing context](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#window-bc), W's [browsing context](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#window-bc), P, and the [current settings object](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#current-settings-object).
